  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 118 AM CDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nAs the trough moves east, ridging extending northeast from the\nwestern US ridge will nudge into the area in its wake. At the\nsurface, high pressure pushes through the Great Lakes Monday and\ninto the Ohio Valley on Tuesday. This puts our area on the \nwestern edge of a cool, continental air mass with winds turning \nfrom northerly to easterly and eventually southerly by midweek. \nDaily sunshine will help to modify the air mass with high \ntemperatures climbing through the 80s to near 90 by Wednesday or\nThursday. Moisture from the Gulf remains blocked by the advance\nof high pressure, so we'll be working with recycled moisture \nlocally with dewpoints likely in the 60s through Wednesday, a \nmodest level of humidity for this time of year.\n\nA trough arriving in the Pacific Northwest this weekend pushes \nback against the Western US ridge and emerges out into the \nPlains by midweek. A shortwave rounding the base of this trough \nbrings our next rain chances as it interacts with the first bit \nof return Gulf moisture Wednesday night into Thursday. Another \nwave quick on its heals produces another round of showers and \nstorms Thursday night into Friday. Where each of these rounds \nforms and tracks will be determined later, but for now at least \ntwo rounds are likely across the region, focused mainly at \nnight. With greater moisture returning to the area, the \npotential for an east-west boundary and some training along it, \nwe will have to monitor this time period for the potential for \nlocally heavy rainfall and flash flooding.\n\nUncertainty on temperatures increases during this time period \nas how warm we get will depend on where a frontal boundary sets \nup and how much cloud cover is around. Guidance which keeps the \nfront further north brings hot temperatures back into the 90s as\nearly as Wednesday. While the probability of 90 degree heat \nincreases each day through next weekend, there's still a lot of \nuncertainty with passing waves each day having some effect on \nthe front's position. NBM interquartile spread for high \ntemperatures is 7 to 10 degrees from Thursday onward, relatively\nhigh spread for this time of year. On the warm side of things, \ntemperatures could rise into the mid 90s with humidity returning\nto push heat index values back over 100. But if the front stays\ncloser to the area then clouds and showers are more likely to \nhold temperatures back several degrees, potentially remaining in\nthe 80s.",
